# Tracer: native Mathlib carriers with uniform Litex Obj identity.
#
# Selected ABI:
#   N/Z/Q/R/C become Set.univ over Mathlib's native carriers;
#   a domain binder becomes a bounded quantifier and keeps its membership fact;
#   symbols and numerals retain their structural source spelling;
#   target coercions occur only at checked judgment boundaries.

# A closed numeral remains bare; R supplies the native target carrier.
2 $in R

# Integer subtraction keeps its source identity and native carrier.
forall z Z:
    z - 1 $in Z

# Integer binary closure is carried by structured verifier evidence. `%` has
# an intrinsic Z result/operand contract rather than inheriting Nat from bare
# Lean numerals.
forall a, b Z:
    a + b $in Z
    a - b $in Z
    a * b $in Z

# The refined binder supplies the exact source proof `b != 0`; the same proof
# is retained in the WD certificate for both occurrences of `a % b`.
forall a Z, b Z*:
    a % b $in Z
    a % b = a % b

# Closed computation is a checked integer-reflection certificate. These two
# examples jointly pin the carrier and the computed result.
5 % 2 = 5 % 2
5 % 2 = 1

# Existing Litex-verified but not-yet-lowered proof families remain explicit
# To-Lean boundaries (comments keep this strict acceptance tracer passing):
# forall n N:
#     n + 1 $in N
#     n + 1 $in Z
# forall z Z:
#     z / 2 $in Q
# forall z Z, q Q:
#     z + q $in Q
#     z / 2 + q $in Q
# forall n N+:
#     n - 1 $in N
# forall n N:
#     n - 1 $in N
#
# The final statement is rejected by Litex itself. A backend must preserve
# that boundary and must not reinterpret it as truncated Nat subtraction.
#
# Source gate:
# target/release/litex -compact -isolated -runner -f examples/05_compiler_interop/compile_to_lean_numeric_obj_abi.lit
#
# Implementation boundary:
# src/compile_to_lean/math_collections.md#numeric-object-abi
